# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Ollive — copy to `.env` and fill in. Every value is read through
# packages/config, which validates the whole environment ONCE at boot and
# exits loudly if anything is missing or malformed.
#
# No service reads process.env directly. That is what makes these services
# container-ready: the same binary, a different environment.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Database ────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Any Postgres 14+. Must include ?sslmode=require for a managed provider.
#
# SUPABASE: use the "Session pooler" connection string, NOT "Direct connection".
#   Direct (db.<ref>.supabase.co) publishes only an IPv6 AAAA record, so it is
#   unreachable from any network without IPv6 — which includes most of Windows.
#   The pooler is dual-stack. Note the username becomes postgres.<project-ref>.
#
#   Use SESSION mode (port 5432), not TRANSACTION mode (6543): the migration
#   runner takes a session-scoped pg_advisory_lock so concurrent deploys cannot
#   migrate at once, and transaction mode supports neither session-scoped
#   advisory locks nor prepared statements.
#
#   postgresql://postgres.<ref>:<pw>@aws-0-<region>.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require
#
# NEON:  postgresql://user:pw@ep-xxx.region.aws.neon.tech/ollive?sslmode=require
DATABASE_URL=postgresql://postgres.xxxx:password@aws-0-ap-south-1.pooler.supabase.com:5432/postgres?sslmode=require

# Verify the server's TLS certificate chain. Left false because managed
# providers present chains absent from Node's bundled CA store, and pg v8 now
# reads sslmode=require as full verification. The connection is encrypted
# regardless; this controls whether the server is authenticated.
DATABASE_SSL_STRICT=false

# ── Model providers ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# At least one is required. The chat app offers whichever ones are keyed.
ANTHROPIC_API_KEY=sk-ant-...
OPENAI_API_KEY=sk-proj-...

# ── Telemetry ingestion ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where the SDK ships its events.
#   local dev  → http://localhost:3001   (the standalone Fastify service)
#   deployed   → https://<your-app>.vercel.app/api   (the in-app route)
#
# If this is UNSET the SDK transport becomes a no-op with a single startup
# warning. It never throws and never retries into the void — the absence of
# telemetry must not break the app being instrumented.
INGEST_ENDPOINT=http://localhost:3001

# Shared secret between SDK and ingestion. Any non-empty string for local dev.
# The SDK preflights this at startup so a bad key fails at boot, not silently
# at runtime three hours later.
INGEST_API_KEY=dev-local-key-change-me

# Port for the standalone Fastify ingestion service.
INGEST_PORT=3001

# ── Event sink ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Where accepted events go.
#
#   direct  await the insert. Ingest latency IS database latency, and a
#           database outage returns 503. Simplest, correct at low volume.
#   pgmq    enqueue and return; an in-process worker persists. A database blip
#           queues work instead of rejecting it. Needs no extra infrastructure
#           — pgmq lives inside Postgres. Requires migration 005.
#   kafka   not implemented. See packages/ingest-core/src/sink.ts for the
#           thresholds at which it would be worth the operational cost.
#
# Safe to switch either way: the persist path is idempotent, so at-least-once
# redelivery from the queue cannot double-count.
INGEST_SINK=direct

# Run the queue worker in the ingestion process. Set false to scale it out
# separately — a deployment change, not a code change.
INGEST_WORKER=true

# ── Telemetry tuning (all optional — defaults shown) ────────────────────────
# Flush when the buffer reaches this many events...
OLLIVE_BATCH_SIZE=50
# ...or when this many milliseconds have passed, whichever comes first.
OLLIVE_FLUSH_MS=200
# Hard cap on the in-memory buffer. When full, the OLDEST events are dropped
# and counted. We never block the caller and never grow without bound.
OLLIVE_MAX_QUEUE=1000
# How many characters of prompt/completion to keep as a preview.
# Full text lives in `messages`; the event table stays narrow so it scans fast.
OLLIVE_PREVIEW_CHARS=300
# Redaction placement: sdk | ingest | both | off
# `both` is defence in depth — redact before the data leaves the process,
# then re-scan centrally as a safety net.
OLLIVE_REDACTION=both

# ── Dashboard (runs on its own host) ────────────────────────────────────────
DASHBOARD_PORT=3002

# Shared bearer token for the metrics API. UNSET MEANS OPEN.
#
# Fine locally. Once the dashboard is on its own host, set it — the dashboard
# exposes spend per model and raw provider error messages. It is a shared
# secret, not authentication: no users, no sessions, no revocation. For
# anything real, put the host behind your identity provider or a VPN.
# DASHBOARD_TOKEN=at-least-eight-characters

# Cross-links between the two UIs. Each is optional; the link is simply hidden
# when unset, which is correct when they are deployed independently and one of
# them is not public.
#
# NEXT_PUBLIC_* is the one documented exception to "nothing reads process.env
# outside packages/config" — Next inlines these at build time so the browser
# can see them, and server-side config is unreachable from client components.
# NEXT_PUBLIC_DASHBOARD_URL=http://localhost:3002
# NEXT_PUBLIC_CHAT_URL=http://localhost:3000

# NOTE: the dashboard reads DATABASE_URL like everything else — but because it
# is a separate deployment, it can be given a DIFFERENT one. Point it at a read
# replica and analytics queries stop competing with the chat app for the
# primary's connections. That is the main reason it is a separate app.

# ── Runtime ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
NODE_ENV=development
LOG_LEVEL=info
